{"custom_id":"s-ap_calculus_ab-f02-k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs5ykjt1eHkTip74hYZ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Part (a): f(1) = 1 - 4 = -3. The right-hand limit is -4(1) + 1 = -3. The two sides agree, so f is continuous at x = 1.\n\nPart (b): For x < 1, f'(x) = 2x - 4, which gives -2 at x = 1. For x > 1, f'(x) = -2/√x, which gives -2 at x = 1. Since f is continuous at x = 1, the one-sided difference quotients both equal -2, so f is differentiable at x = 1 and f'(1) = -2.\n\nPart (c): The tangent line through (1, -3) with slope -2 is y = -3 - 2(x - 1).\n\nPart (d): f(4) = -8 + 1 = -7 and f(0) = 0. The average value is (f(4) - f(0))/4 = -7/4."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U2KZX3jjKmhShTF5pG2RWD","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"a","brief_reasoning":"f(1)=-3; left limit -3; right limit -4+1=-3; equal so continuous.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"final_answer":"lim x→1 f(x) = -3 = f(1), so f is continuous at x=1"},{"item":"b","brief_reasoning":"Left derivative 2(1)-4=-2; right derivative -2/sqrt(1)=-2; continuous, so differentiable.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"final_answer":"Yes, f'(1) = -2"},{"item":"c","brief_reasoning":"Point (1,-3), slope -2.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"final_answer":"y = -3 - 2(x-1)"},{"item":"d","brief_reasoning":"(f(4)-f(0))/4 = (-7-0)/4.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"final_answer":"-7/4"}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":489,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":655,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":489,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"eph